---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";

const title = "Treadmill Making a Squeaking Noise? (2026): Locate It by Sound";
const description =
  "Where the noise comes from, whether it tracks belt speed, and whether it changes with your weight on it — three questions that narrow a squeak to one component.";

const NOISES = [
  {
    noise: "Rhythmic squeak, once per belt rotation",
    source: "The belt seam or a damaged belt spot",
    fix: "Inspect the belt for a lifted seam or a worn patch.",
  },
  {
    noise: "Squeal from the front, rises with speed",
    source: "Front roller bearing or drive belt",
    fix: "Check tension first — over-tight belts kill front bearings.",
  },
  {
    noise: "Squeak only when you step on it",
    source: "Deck, frame joint or cushioning element",
    fix: "Usually loose bolts. Check every fastener on the frame.",
  },
  {
    noise: "Dull rubbing or shuffling, speed-related",
    source: "Dry deck or a belt touching the frame",
    fix: "Lubricate, then check the belt is centred.",
  },
  {
    noise: "Squeak or clunk on incline changes only",
    source: "Incline motor or its lifting linkage",
    fix: "Separate system entirely. Check the linkage pivots.",
  },
  {
    noise: "Grinding or rumbling, worsening weekly",
    source: "A bearing on its way out",
    fix: "Not a squeak. Stop using it and get it looked at.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Guides", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/best-of/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Treadmill Squeaking Noise", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/treadmill-squeaking-noise/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Why is my treadmill squeaking?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The usual sources are a roller bearing, loose frame or deck bolts, the belt seam passing over a roller, and the deck cushioning elements. Three questions narrow it down: where the sound comes from, whether it speeds up with the belt, and whether it only happens with your weight on the machine.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Should I use WD-40 on a squeaking treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No. WD-40 and other petroleum-based sprays damage the deck surface and the belt backing, and they attract dust that makes matters worse. The only lubricant that belongs on a treadmill deck is 100 per cent silicone treadmill lubricant. Pivot points on some frames take a different lubricant — check the manual rather than guessing.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is a squeaking treadmill dangerous?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Most squeaks are not, but some are early warnings. A noise that has been getting steadily worse, a grinding or rumbling rather than a squeak, or any noise accompanied by a burning smell or heat means stop using the machine. Those point at a bearing failing or a motor overheating rather than at something needing tightening.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Why does my treadmill squeak only when I walk on it?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A noise that appears only under load is almost always structural rather than rotational. Loose frame bolts, deck mounting hardware, worn cushioning elements and the folding hinge on a folding machine all move slightly under body weight and stop when you step off. Check and re-torque every accessible bolt before looking further.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can a loose treadmill belt cause squeaking?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A loose belt slipping on the front roller can produce a squeal at start-up or under load. An over-tight belt is the more common noise source, because the extra tension loads the roller bearings and that is what produces a rising squeal or rumble at the front of the machine. Check the lift at the deck centre before adjusting anything.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How do I stop my treadmill from squeaking?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Work in order: check the deck is lubricated, check belt tension and tracking, re-torque every accessible frame and deck bolt, then check the levelling feet. Those four cover most squeaks. What remains is usually a bearing or an incline linkage, and those are service jobs rather than owner adjustments.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Morgan Reyes" },
      datePublished: "2026-09-06",
      dateModified: "2026-09-06",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" },
    },
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | Treadmill Reviews USA`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-09-06",
    modifiedTime: "2026-09-06",
    author: "Morgan Reyes",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: [
      "treadmill squeaking",
      "treadmill noise",
      "treadmill roller bearing",
      "treadmill troubleshooting",
      "home gym",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <svg class="absolute inset-0 w-full h-full opacity-10" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
        <pattern id="dot-pattern-squeak" x="0" y="0" width="24" height="24" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse">
          <circle cx="2" cy="2" r="1.5" fill="white"></circle>
        </pattern>
        <rect width="100%" height="100%" fill="url(#dot-pattern-squeak)"></rect>
      </svg>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center gap-3 mb-6 flex-wrap">
          <span class="bg-[#FF5A1F] text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Troubleshooting
          </span>
          <span class="bg-green-500 text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Updated September 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Treadmill Making a <span class="text-[#5AA9FF]">Squeaking Noise</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
          Three questions locate almost any treadmill noise: where is it coming
          from, does it speed up with the belt, and does it only happen with your
          weight on the machine. Answer those and you have narrowed six possible
          sources to one.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-4">
          <a href="#locate" class="bg-[#FF5A1F] hover:bg-[#E64F16] text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm">
            Locate the Noise
          </a>
          <a href="#serious" class="bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm border border-white/20">
            Noises That Mean Stop
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4 py-8 border-b border-gray-100">
        <img src="https://i.pravatar.cc/150?img=48" alt="Morgan Reyes" class="w-12 h-12 rounded-full" />
        <div>
          <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 leading-tight">Morgan Reyes</p>
          <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm leading-tight">Fitness Equipment Editor</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block w-px h-8 bg-gray-200"></div>
        <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm">
          Last Updated: <strong class="text-gray-700">September 6, 2026</strong>
        </p>
        <div class="ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="4.3k" />
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="not-prose my-10 bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 m-0 leading-relaxed">
          <strong class="text-red-900">Never spray WD-40 or household oil on a
          treadmill deck.</strong> Petroleum-based products degrade the deck surface
          and the belt backing and attract dust that makes the noise worse. The only
          thing that belongs on a deck is 100 per cent silicone treadmill lubricant.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-2 text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mt-0 mb-4">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-5 h-5 text-[#FF5A1F]" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M4 6h16M4 12h16M4 18h7"></path></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#three-questions" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">1. The Three Questions</a>
          <a href="#locate" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">2. Noise-to-Source Table</a>
          <a href="#bolts" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">3. Loose Bolts Come First</a>
          <a href="#rollers" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">4. Roller Bearings</a>
          <a href="#belt-noises" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">5. Belt and Deck Noises</a>
          <a href="#incline" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">6. Incline Motor Noises</a>
          <a href="#wrong-lubricants" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">7. Lubricants That Cause Damage</a>
          <a href="#serious" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">8. Noises That Mean Stop</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">9. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Most treadmill squeaks are loose hardware, and most of the rest are a
          bearing telling you something well before it fails. The difficulty is that
          a squeak travels through a steel frame and seems to come from everywhere,
          so people replace the wrong part. Locating it properly takes about five
          minutes and needs nothing but attention.
        </p>

        <div id="three-questions" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. The Three Questions</h2>
          <p>
            Answer these before touching anything. Together they eliminate most of
            the candidate list.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Does the noise track belt speed?</strong> Run the machine empty
            and change the speed. A noise that gets faster as the belt gets faster is
            rotational — a roller, a bearing, the drive belt, or the belt seam. A
            noise that stays the same, or only appears at particular speeds, is
            usually structural or a resonance.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Does it need your weight?</strong> Run it empty, then walk on it
            slowly. A noise that only appears when you step on is structural: frame
            bolts, deck mounting hardware, cushioning elements, or a folding hinge.
            One that is identical empty and loaded is rotational.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Where is it, front or rear?</strong> This is harder than it
            sounds, because steel carries sound extremely well. Have someone else
            walk on the machine while you crouch at each end in turn, or use a long
            screwdriver as a stethoscope — handle to your ear, tip against the frame
            near each roller housing, with the belt running empty. Keep clear of
            moving parts.
          </p>
          <p>
            One more piece of information: how long it has been happening, and
            whether it is getting worse. A stable noise that has been there for
            months is usually cosmetic. A noise that has developed over a few weeks
            and is worsening is a component degrading, and that changes the urgency.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="locate" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. Noise-to-Source Table</h2>
          <p>
            Match what you are hearing to the closest row. The distinctions between
            these are small and the causes are quite different.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="not-prose my-8 overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="min-w-[680px] w-full text-sm border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-gray-900 text-white">
            <tr>
              <th class="text-left px-4 py-3 font-black">What You Hear</th>
              <th class="text-left px-4 py-3 font-black">Likely Source</th>
              <th class="text-left px-4 py-3 font-black">What to Do</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              NOISES.map((row, i) => (
                <tr class={i % 2 === 0 ? "bg-white" : "bg-gray-50"}>
                  <td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900 align-top">{row.noise}</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 align-top">{row.source}</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-500 align-top">{row.fix}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          Note the difference between the fourth row and the last. A dull rubbing
          sound is friction and is fixed with a bottle of silicone. A grinding or
          rumbling that has been getting worse week by week is a bearing, and it is
          the one noise on this page that warrants stopping rather than
          investigating.
        </p>

        <div id="bolts" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Loose Bolts Come First</h2>
          <p>
            The most common cause of a treadmill squeak, the cheapest to fix, and the
            one people skip because it seems too simple.
          </p>
          <p>
            A treadmill is a bolted steel structure that gets shaken thousands of
            times per session. Fasteners work loose, and once a joint has a fraction
            of a millimetre of play in it, two pieces of metal rub every time your
            foot lands. That produces a squeak or a creak that appears under load and
            vanishes when you step off.
          </p>
          <p>
            Unplug the machine and go round every accessible bolt with the supplied
            hex keys and a spanner. The usual offenders are the upright-to-frame
            bolts at the base of the console mast, the handrail fixings, the side
            rail screws, the motor hood screws, and the deck mounting hardware.
            Snug rather than forced: over-torquing into a plastic or thin steel
            component causes its own problems.
          </p>
          <p>
            Pay particular attention to <strong>folding machines</strong>. The
            hinge, the latch and the gas strut mounting are all high-stress joints
            that flex every time the machine folds, and they are the single most
            common squeak source on a folding treadmill. Some hinges have a
            specified lubricant; check the manual rather than reaching for whatever
            is in the cupboard.
          </p>
          <p>
            Also check the levelling feet. A machine that rocks slightly on an uneven
            floor flexes its frame with every footstrike, and that flex creaks. Get a
            spirit level across the frame and wind the rear feet until it sits flat
            and does not move when you push a corner — the same check that fixes
            belt tracking, described in
            <a href="/how-to-center-treadmill-belt/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how to centre a treadmill belt</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="rollers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Roller Bearings</h2>
          <p>
            The second most common source, and the one worth taking seriously because
            it usually has a preventable cause.
          </p>
          <p>
            Front and rear rollers run on sealed bearings. When one starts to fail it
            produces a squeal or rumble that <strong>rises and falls with belt
            speed</strong> and is loudest at the end of the machine where the bad
            bearing sits. It is present with the belt running empty, which
            distinguishes it from the structural noises above.
          </p>
          <p>
            The most common cause is belt over-tension. Tension is a compressive load
            carried directly by the roller bearings, and a belt tightened well past
            correct shortens their life considerably. This is why the first thing to
            check on a front-end squeal is not the bearing but the belt: with the
            machine off, you should be able to lift the belt two to three inches at
            the centre of the deck. If you cannot, back both rear bolts off equally —
            the method is in
            <a href="/how-tight-should-treadmill-belt-be/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how tight a treadmill belt should be</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            Loosening the belt to correct will not repair a bearing that has already
            been damaged, but it stops the damage progressing, and it occasionally
            resolves a noise that was tension rather than wear. It is a free thing to
            try before spending anything.
          </p>
          <p>
            A confirmed bad bearing is a replacement roller on most home machines,
            since consumer rollers usually have pressed-in sealed bearings that are
            not separately serviceable. It is a straightforward job for a technician
            and a moderate one for a confident owner, and it is worth doing rather
            than living with, because a failing bearing adds drag that the motor pays
            for continuously.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="belt-noises" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Belt and Deck Noises</h2>
          <p>
            Three distinct sounds come from the belt and the surface it runs on, and
            they are easy to confuse with each other.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A rhythmic tick or squeak once per rotation</strong> is the belt
            itself. Every running belt has a seam, and a seam that has begun to lift
            or has hardened makes a noise each time it passes over a roller. Unplug
            and inspect the belt all the way round, looking for a raised seam, a worn
            patch, or a nick in the surface. A lifting seam is a belt approaching the
            end of its life.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A dull rubbing or shuffling that rises with speed</strong> is
            usually a dry deck. Friction produces a broad, soft noise quite unlike a
            squeak, and it comes with the other dry-deck symptoms — a belt that
            hesitates under your weight, a hotter motor hood. Run a finger across the
            deck under the belt at the centre: slick is fine, dry or grippy means
            lubricate. The interval is in
            <a href="/how-often-to-lubricate-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how often to lubricate a treadmill</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A scraping or hissing from one side</strong> is a belt riding
            against the frame or the side rail. Look at the gap on each side of the
            belt while it runs empty; if it is much wider on one side, the belt has
            drifted off centre and is grinding its own edge away. That is worth
            fixing the day you notice it, because it destroys a belt in weeks. The
            correction is in
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-slipping/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt slipping and drifting</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            One more: some machines have elastomer cushioning elements under the deck
            that harden with age and squeak under load. If the noise is under your
            feet, only under load, and every bolt is tight, this is the likely
            remaining candidate.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="incline" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Incline Motor Noises</h2>
          <p>
            A separate system with its own noises, and the one people misattribute
            most often.
          </p>
          <p>
            The incline mechanism is a small motor driving a lead screw or an
            actuator that lifts the front of the frame. It is loud by design — a
            whirring or grinding noise while the deck is moving is normal on almost
            every machine, and it stops when the incline reaches the set grade.
          </p>
          <p>
            What is not normal is a squeak or a clunk that happens during incline
            changes and is new. That usually points at the pivot points of the
            lifting linkage, where the frame moves against the base. Those pivots
            take significant load and some are designed to be lubricated
            periodically, though not with the silicone that goes on the deck. Check
            the manual for what it specifies.
          </p>
          <p>
            A grinding from the incline motor itself, or an incline that no longer
            reaches its set grade, or a machine that reports an incline error, is a
            service issue. It is also worth taking seriously because the incline
            mechanism carries the weight of the deck and the user.
          </p>
          <p>
            Test it in isolation: with the machine on but the belt stopped, run the
            incline up and down through its range and listen. That completely
            separates incline noises from belt noises, and it is the quickest way to
            rule the system in or out. Machines that spend most of their life at
            grade — for the
            <a href="/12-3-30-treadmill-workout/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">12-3-30 workout</a>,
            for example — put more cycles through this mechanism than average and are
            worth checking annually.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="wrong-lubricants" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Lubricants That Cause Damage</h2>
          <p>
            The instinct on hearing a squeak is to spray something at it, and on a
            treadmill that instinct causes more damage than the squeak ever would.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>WD-40 and penetrating oils</strong> are the most common mistake.
            They are solvents rather than lubricants, they degrade the deck surface
            and the belt backing, and they attract dust that turns into an abrasive
            paste. A deck that has been sprayed with WD-40 is frequently ruined, and
            it is not recoverable by cleaning.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Household oils, cooking oils and general-purpose greases</strong>
            do not belong anywhere on a running deck either. They do not have the
            right viscosity, they oxidise, and they make an even better dust magnet
            than a solvent does.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Silicone sprays containing propellants or carriers</strong> are a
            subtler trap, because the label says silicone. What goes on a deck is
            100 per cent silicone treadmill lubricant, applied as a liquid under the
            belt — not sprayed at a distance, and not a product whose main ingredient
            is a solvent that carries a little silicone.
          </p>
          <p>
            The one legitimate exception is frame pivots and folding hinges, some of
            which the manufacturer does specify a lubricant for. That is a
            documented instruction in the manual for a specific point, not
            permission to spray the machine generally. When in doubt, tighten it
            rather than lubricate it — the correct application procedure for the
            deck is in the
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-lubrication/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">lubrication guide</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="serious" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Noises That Mean Stop</h2>
          <p>
            Most squeaks are an annoyance. A few are a warning, and the difference is
            worth knowing before you spend an evening tightening bolts.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Stop using the machine</strong> if the noise comes with a burning
            or hot-plastic smell, if the breaker trips, if the belt keeps moving after
            the safety key is pulled, or if the machine surges. Those are electrical
            and safety-system faults rather than noise problems, and they are covered
            in the
            <a href="/treadmill-troubleshooting/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">troubleshooting guide</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Stop and investigate before the next session</strong> if the noise
            is a grinding or a rumble rather than a squeak, if it has been getting
            steadily worse over a few weeks, or if it is accompanied by any change in
            how the belt feels underfoot. Those describe a bearing degrading, and a
            bearing that seizes at speed stops the belt abruptly with someone on it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Investigate at your convenience</strong> if the noise is stable,
            appears only under load, and nothing else about the machine has changed.
            That is almost certainly hardware that needs tightening, and it will keep
            until the weekend.
          </p>
          <p>
            The preventive version of this page is short: keep the deck lubricated,
            keep the belt at correct tension rather than tight, and go round the bolts
            once a year. Those three habits eliminate most of what is on this page
            before it starts, and they are part of the
            <a href="/treadmill-maintenance/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">maintenance schedule</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why is my treadmill squeaking?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The usual sources are a roller bearing, loose frame or deck bolts, the
                belt seam passing over a roller, and the deck cushioning elements.
                Three questions narrow it down: where the sound comes from, whether it
                speeds up with the belt, and whether it only happens with your weight
                on the machine.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I use WD-40 on a squeaking treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. WD-40 and other petroleum-based sprays damage the deck surface and
                the belt backing, and they attract dust that makes matters worse. The
                only lubricant that belongs on a treadmill deck is 100 per cent
                silicone treadmill lubricant. Pivot points on some frames take a
                different lubricant — check the manual rather than guessing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a squeaking treadmill dangerous?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most squeaks are not, but some are early warnings. A noise that has
                been getting steadily worse, a grinding or rumbling rather than a
                squeak, or any noise accompanied by a burning smell or heat means stop
                using the machine. Those point at a bearing failing or a motor
                overheating rather than at something needing tightening.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my treadmill squeak only when I walk on it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A noise that appears only under load is almost always structural rather
                than rotational. Loose frame bolts, deck mounting hardware, worn
                cushioning elements and the folding hinge on a folding machine all move
                slightly under body weight and stop when you step off. Check and
                re-torque every accessible bolt before looking further.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a loose treadmill belt cause squeaking?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A loose belt slipping on the front roller can produce a squeal at
                start-up or under load. An over-tight belt is the more common noise
                source, because the extra tension loads the roller bearings and that is
                what produces a rising squeal or rumble at the front of the machine.
                Check the lift at the deck centre before adjusting anything.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I stop my treadmill from squeaking?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Work in order: check the deck is lubricated, check belt tension and
                tracking, re-torque every accessible frame and deck bolt, then check
                the levelling feet. Those four cover most squeaks. What remains is
                usually a bearing or an incline linkage, and those are service jobs
                rather than owner adjustments.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Chasing a Treadmill Noise</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">The first of these has ended more decks than any other single maintenance error.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Spraying WD-40 at the noise</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">It is a solvent, not a lubricant. On a deck it degrades the running surface and the belt backing, and it attracts dust that becomes an abrasive paste. A deck sprayed with it is frequently beyond recovery. Only 100 per cent silicone treadmill lubricant belongs on a deck, applied as a liquid under the belt.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming a front-end squeal is a bad bearing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Belt tension is a compressive load carried directly by the roller bearings, and over-tightening is the usual reason they start to complain. Check the lift at the deck centre before pricing a roller: if you cannot raise the belt two inches, the tension is the problem and correcting it stops the damage progressing.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the bolts because it seems too simple</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Loose hardware is the single most common cause of a treadmill squeak, and a noise that appears only under your weight is structural until proven otherwise. Go round the upright bolts, handrail fixings, rail screws and deck mounts before investigating anything rotational — and on a folding machine, check the hinge and latch first.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Living with a noise that is getting worse</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A stable squeak can wait for the weekend. A grinding or rumbling that has worsened over a few weeks is a bearing degrading, and a bearing that seizes at speed stops the belt abruptly with someone standing on it. Any noise with a burning smell, a tripping breaker or a change in belt feel means stop now.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Related Guides -->
        <div class="mt-16">
